#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484350} :activity/id #uuid "774a94cd-8530-573f-80d6-592f51a33b36" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/id #uuid "d31d3400-00a2-5388-a09e-ece03d04c735" :topic/math-academy-id 2623} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483598} {:answer/id #uuid "4c340483-1504-5c29-8184-e2464a93b17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483708} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecb7b58-6f98-517c-8a10-0c822f40ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483826} {:answer/id #uuid "b17ad927-4efe-5a54-8fec-c33efe368201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483920} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f2c10c-efb5-5f13-bc42-613ef41e630b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483931}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483598} :answer-field/id #uuid "64759caf-77ed-5792-a395-f9662dbea5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" :question/math-academy-id "q-75951" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "0b8b441f-1ac7-578f-9670-a1e7fdce2cfe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484311}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e3091b-8980-52e7-a1d9-a90be4aa96ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} {:answer/id #uuid "3d86af19-6bb9-5da7-832b-79a566c84f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423605} {:answer/id #uuid "67c52e20-2bf3-5d92-86ef-d45fa30e8513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423606} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2e8bb6-fedc-5092-b791-68f16dc74270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423611} {:answer/id #uuid "a9845672-a026-53af-b2ba-1e8d7af5f91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423604} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f4cbcc-1506-5396-b835-9d7d9a6a3ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "693e6ed3-909b-52a6-8fa3-e0e6ae013327" :question/math-academy-id "q-75982" :question/problem "The matrix $F$ is given below. Let ${f}_{1}$, ${f}_{2}$,  ${f}_{3}$ and ${f}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $F.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${f}_{4}={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2}+{x}_{3}{f}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $F$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & -24 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(F)$ is $\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{f}_{1},{f}_{2},{f}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(F).$"} :step/id #uuid "17548c6c-a0db-59ae-8691-b5c2e89ec05c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484323}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484311 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3214ac66-089e-5f1a-b5f5-27127d89af81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342662} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6f8a38-0a5a-5b8d-9bbd-8d6b55a7767b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343032} {:answer/id #uuid "69df0ecf-34a7-5ab2-a85a-47fd55e5f70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343142} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9fef81-c774-530d-bce3-3ec15ebb79e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344469} {:answer/id #uuid "f635b87e-9ad6-595e-8ac8-cf7446521d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423617} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab78a2e2-73c6-516c-bb41-33e676435ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75980" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$"} :step/id #uuid "2285bbbb-2b94-5bc1-a8a2-c4f8628b9d46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ca77f7-231e-5f3a-9af5-87ee3086ded9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483660} {:answer/id #uuid "369b1b63-2671-515f-a882-54df1b9bcd81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483666} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee1b5e6-8510-51f6-b77d-9e89493cdd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483686} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa6abc-3795-555d-a118-02cf871c6f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483762} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f26072-e997-52aa-9840-7944fc90bd14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483926} :answer-field/id #uuid "572b5c18-b30c-522b-af06-1c2750b42b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb27e567-0848-5034-b8d8-32b6b421d46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-111504" :question/problem "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$, and ${h}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${h}_{2}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{3}{h}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is $\\{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$"} :step/id #uuid "57e1f077-3a25-541d-a79a-09e739748679"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26229f41-5686-5fd3-9b06-e60b8419a926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483631} {:answer/id #uuid "26842599-1eb5-50e0-859b-db04ef0c0686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483633} {:answer/id #uuid "73e827cb-d4df-51d5-a0c2-8e504741edcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483799} {:answer/id #uuid "857b74b9-1552-5629-a376-93f606672083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483836} {:answer/id #uuid "e01b0506-44a8-5c88-a48e-942f949bf680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484014}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483631} :answer-field/id #uuid "259644ac-e832-5d7b-b706-9bf284412c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88299f24-4f45-5ffe-aaf6-eeca4cfa5124" :question/math-academy-id "q-75089" :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$,  ${p}_{3}$ and ${p}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $P.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{4}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{4}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "c7e441a7-a721-5e10-85ac-7c952dabd92c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484303}}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483560}]}
